Under **NC Medicaid Clinical Coverage Policy 10A**, outpatient therapy services must be provided under a **treatment plan (plan of care)** that includes all of the following:

- the **start and end date** of the therapy treatment plan, with a duration of **no more than six months**;
- the **discipline-specific treatment diagnosis** and any related medical diagnoses;
- the beneficiary’s **rehabilitative or habilitative potential**;
- **defined goals** for each therapeutic discipline that are **specific, measurable, and reasonably expected to be achieved** within the plan period;
- the **skilled interventions, methodology, procedures, and specific programs** to be used;
- the **frequency of services**;
- the **length of each treatment visit in minutes**; and
- the **name, credentials, and signature** of the professional completing the treatment plan, **dated on or before the plan start date**.  
  [[Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment)]

Related requirements in Policy 10A also say:

- **All services must be provided according to a treatment plan** that meets Subsection **3.2.1.6**.
- Providers must **review and renew or revise treatment plans and goals at least every six calendar months**.
- The beneficiary record must include **a copy of the treatment plan**.  
  [[Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment)]

**Bottom line:** NC Medicaid Policy 10A requires a written plan of care with specific content, signed and dated by the responsible professional on or before the start date, lasting no longer than six months, and reviewed/renewed or revised at least every six months. The current Policy 10A in the corpus is effective **February 1, 2025**, and I did not find any separate amendment notice superseding it.

Under CAP/DA, home modifications must be tied to the beneficiary’s assessed needs and person-centered service plan, and must be aimed at improving mobility, safety, independence, community integration, environmental/community accessibility, or addressing 24/7 coverage concerns. The beneficiary also agrees to work with the multidisciplinary team to identify needs for equipment, modification, and technology. Current authority is the effective 9/1/2025 version of [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), and I did not find any separate amending change notice through the amendments tool.

## Requirements for home modifications under CAP/DA

### General coverage purpose
Equipment, modification, and technology services must serve one or more of these purposes:

- improve, maximize, or enhance the beneficiary’s mobility, safety, or independence;
- improve integration into the community;
- improve the beneficiary’s environmental and community accessibility; or
- address 24-hours-a-day, 7-days-a-week coverage concerns.  
  ([Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 3.4.5, effective 9/1/2025)

### Collaboration with the MDT
The policy states that the beneficiary agrees to:

- “Work collaboratively with my multidisciplinary team to identify my needs for equipment, modification, technology, training and education and goods and services.”  
  ([Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), “I agree to:” section, effective 9/1/2025)

### What kinds of home modifications are contemplated
The policy’s equipment/modification section includes, among other examples:

- ramps;
- grab bars, safety rails, and handrails;
- widening doorways or passages for wheelchair or walker access;
- emergency egress when medically necessary due to physical limitations of the responsible party;
- bathroom accessibility modifications;
- bedroom modifications to accommodate hospital beds and wheelchairs;
- kitchen accessibility modifications;
- floor coverings for ease of ambulation;
- certain lifts, door modifications, and environmental control devices;
- replacement of fixtures, mirrors, and storage space when removed or damaged during an approved modification.  
  ([Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), “EQUIPMENT, MODIFICAITON AND TECHNOLOGY,” effective 9/1/2025)

### Residence limitations
The section indicates modifications may apply to:

- the beneficiary’s primary private residence if owned by the beneficiary or family;
- a rented residence if the modifications are portable; or
- a rented residence if the owner allows the modifications.  
  ([Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), “EQUIPMENT, MODIFICAITON AND TECHNOLOGY,” effective 9/1/2025)

### Limits/exclusions relevant to modifications
The policy also says CAP/DA does **not** cover:

- items not of direct medical or remedial benefit to the beneficiary;
- items covered through the Medicaid State Plan DME/orthotics/prosthetics/home health supply benefit;
- recreational items;
- items of general utility to a non-disabled person;
- warranties and certain maintenance contracts unrelated to the approved service;
- some other excluded items such as swimming pool/spa equipment and unapproved outdoor monitoring systems.  
  ([Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), “Limits, Amount And Frequency,” effective 9/1/2025)

## Documentation the Multidisciplinary Team must provide

The clearest documentation rule is in § 5.9.1. For a home modification request, the MDT documentation must include:

- **a recommendation by the MDT** identifying the beneficiary’s need for the home modification requested;
- **the cost** of the item or modification required;
- **a training plan** for the beneficiary and family on use of the equipment, with completion documented by the case manager and signed by the beneficiary or responsible party;
- **a statement of need in the service plan**;
- **evidence of medical need** recommended by the MDT or by a physician, when applicable;
- **itemized shipping costs** in the proposal request;
- **other information required for the specific equipment requested**;
- **quotes**, when required for purchase, adaptation, or modification, in the number NC Medicaid requires; and
- any **additional written recommendations from appropriate professionals** when NC Medicaid or its designee requires them because of special circumstances.  
  ([Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 5.9.1, effective 9/1/2025)

## Additional documentation requirements appearing elsewhere in the policy

The supporting sections add these items:

- **a comprehensive multidisciplinary declaration-of-need assessment** completed by the case manager identifying equipment, supply, adaptation, or modification needs;
- **a copy of the physician’s order**, when applicable;
- **a recommendation by an appropriate professional** identifying the beneficiary’s needs regarding the requested equipment, supply, adaptation, or modification;
- **the estimated life of the equipment** and how long the beneficiary is expected to benefit from it; and
- **a supplier invoice** showing the date provided, cost, and related charges, maintained in the CAP Business system.  
  ([Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), “Limits, Amount And Frequency,” effective 9/1/2025)

## Bottom line
For CAP/DA home modifications, the key standard is that the modification must be tied to the beneficiary’s assessed needs and service plan and must improve accessibility, safety, independence, or related coverage needs. The MDT must document the beneficiary’s need, medical basis when applicable, cost, service-plan need, training plan, and any required professional recommendations and quotes. Supporting policy text also calls for a declaration-of-need assessment, physician order when applicable, expected useful life/benefit period, and supplier invoice.  

