NC Medicaid does **not** cover certain ambulance services specifically listed in Clinical Coverage Policy 15, and it also excludes ambulance services when the general coverage criteria are not met.

## Specifically not covered ambulance services
Under **Section 4.2.1.4**, NC Medicaid does **not** cover:  
- an ambulance call where **no treatment is needed**;
- a **false alarm** response;
- situations where the **beneficiary refuses all medical services**;
- ambulance transport for a **medical service that is not itself a Medicaid-covered service**;
- **commercial airline tickets**;
- **airstrip fees**;
- **taxes** charged separately (local, state, federal, etc.);
- **separate additional charges for nursing personnel** who are employees of a facility or ambulance service;
- **waiting fees**;
- **separate charges for oxygen or other items/supplies**, because those are included in the base rate; and
- **services other than those listed in Section 3.2**. [Ambulance Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/15-ambulance-services/download?attachment) (effective 12/15/2023), §§ 4.2.1.4, 4.2.1.

## Other ambulance-related limits
NC Medicaid also does **not** reimburse:
- **loaded mileage to a facility that is not the nearest appropriate facility**.  
A hospital is not made “appropriate” just because a physician has staff privileges there, and beneficiary preference alone does not determine whether a facility is appropriate. [Ambulance Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/15-ambulance-services/download?attachment) (effective 12/15/2023), § 4.2.1.

## General reasons ambulance services may be denied
Even beyond the ambulance-specific exclusions, Medicaid does not cover the service if:
- the beneficiary does **not meet eligibility requirements** in Section 2.0;
- the beneficiary does **not meet the coverage criteria** in Section 3.0;
- the service **duplicates another provider’s service**; or
- the service is **experimental, investigational, or part of a clinical trial**. [Ambulance Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/15-ambulance-services/download?attachment) (effective 12/15/2023), § 4.1.

## Coverage rule in the background
The policy’s covered-service section says emergency ambulance services are covered to the extent necessary to **screen and stabilize** the beneficiary, and emergency services are evaluated under the prudent-layperson standard. So if the situation falls outside those covered criteria, it may also be non-covered. [Ambulance Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/15-ambulance-services/download?attachment) (effective 12/15/2023), § 3.2.

