Under NC Medicaid, an HIV case management provider must meet the policy’s certification standards and continue to satisfy them to stay certified. A provider can be decertified for a fairly broad set of compliance, staffing, documentation, billing, and operational failures.

## Certification requirements

NC Medicaid’s HIV Case Management policy requires the provider to:

- have a **documented successful record of three years** providing or managing HIV case management programs;
- ensure HIV case management services are delivered by **qualified case managers** meeting the policy’s staff qualifications;
- ensure HIV case managers are supervised by **qualified supervisors** meeting the policy’s supervision standards;
- **enroll each physical site** with NC Medicaid as an HIV Case Management provider;
- meet **applicable state and federal Medicaid participation laws**;
- **maintain certification** as a qualified HIV Case Management provider;
- demonstrate compliance with **initial and ongoing certification processes**;
- demonstrate compliance with **monitoring and evaluation of case management records through a quality improvement plan**;
- allow NC Medicaid or its designated contractor to **review beneficiary records** and inspect agency operations and financial records;
- **notify NC Medicaid or its designated contractor** of proposed changes—such as ownership, business name, address, phone number, or dissolution—**within 30 calendar days of the proposed change and no later than 5 business days after the actual change**; and
- **achieve national accreditation within one year of Medicaid enrollment** from one of the designated accrediting bodies listed in the policy.  
  Citation: [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 6.2.1, effective 2020-01-15.

### Recertification

For recertification, the provider must:

- submit a **complete and signed Recertification Application** within **60 calendar days of receipt**;
- submit copies of all items from initial certification that have changed;
- submit copies of all HIV case manager and supervisor credentials;
- submit annual summaries of **quality improvement activities**, including outcome metrics; and
- submit to a **recertification on-site visit**, including review of beneficiary records and other clinical or business documentation as needed.  
  Citation: [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 6.2.4, effective 2020-01-15.

### What the recertification checklist specifically looks for

The HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist indicates NC Medicaid expects the recertification package to show, among other things:

- only one physical location per application;
- a signed checklist/title page;
- lists of all **case managers** and **supervisors** with dates of employment;
- all **counties served**;
- all services provided by the agency;
- operating hours and an **emergency after-hours plan**;
- accrediting agency name and dates, or an explanation;
- frequency of **satisfaction surveys** and how results are used; and
- counts of Medicaid and Ryan White clients.  
  Citation: [dma-3157 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3157.pdf).

## When a provider can be decertified

NC Medicaid may decertify an HIV case management provider for any of the following reasons:

- failure to provide the **core service components**;
- **fraudulent billing practices**;
- owner conviction of a **felony**;
- failure to develop, submit, and implement a **written plan of correction** for unmet program requirements within **30 calendar days**;
- **falsification of records**;
- violation of a beneficiary’s **confidentiality**;
- employing staff who do not meet the policy’s qualification standards;
- failure of staff to complete required **basic training within 90 days of employment**;
- failure of staff to obtain required **continuing education units**;
- failure to provide staff supervision sufficient to meet beneficiaries’ needs;
- failure to submit required documentation within the time frame required by policy, NC Medicaid, or its designated contractor;
- failure to maintain documentation sufficient to support billing, as required by the policy;
- failure to implement and enforce a **quality improvement program**;
- failure to timely notify NC Medicaid or its designated contractor of changes in agency name, director, ownership, mailing address, or phone number, resulting in inability to contact the provider;
- failure to comply with **applicable federal and state laws, regulations, Medicaid plan requirements, and policies**;
- failure to enroll any beneficiaries within **4 months of certification**; and
- failure to achieve or maintain the policy’s certification requirements.  
  Citation: [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 6.2.5, effective 2020-01-15.

## Bottom line

A provider must show organizational experience, qualified staff and supervision, site enrollment, accreditation, compliance systems, quality improvement, record access, and timely notice of business changes. Decertification can result not only from fraud or criminal issues, but also from deficient staffing, training, supervision, documentation, billing support, quality improvement, notice failures, and general noncompliance with Medicaid rules.

